    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "They can't be vetted, we found out. And the New York State does everything you shouldn't do, issues drivers licenses, gives protection to illegal migrants, displaces veterans and other our own homeless citizens who don't have a place to go themselves. These are real bad policies and people are starting to wake up to the fact, look, I've had people in my district which is sprawls all the way from the northeast border of Lake Ontario in Clayton all the way to Lockport and including the Finger Lakes, send me video of their ring doorbells and their trail cam showing illegal immigrants all walking around in our district. So these things are happening. These people could be dangerous. And we know that some of them have been dangerous as you as it happened in Buffalo. We had rape accusations. We had a murder in Rensselaer County. We've had a lot of terrible things happen. When you have no control of your border, you're undermining citizenship and you're undermining the taxpayers who are being forced in a very difficult economic situation that we all face today as Americans to now have to pay for even more people who many of whom may end up becoming dependents and not actually working for us. So these are huge problems that the Democrats are refusing to address. It's something we're going to address when we go back to Washington this week. And it's going to be a decisive issue when it comes to the election of twenty twenty four for congress and for president united states especially you know we talk so much about the the border and we have to get the border under control but when in if that ever happens what about the people who are already in this country what is what is the plan for the people we don't know are here Well, interesting that when I was in my first term in Congress, we actually came out with a bill that would prioritize who has to be deported. And there is a substantial majority that needs to be deported sent back to their country of origin. And we're going to have to do that. And we're going to have to decide who was actually born in the United States under that situation, under that law that's been kind of subject to interpretation. But nonetheless, it's been the tradition that we've had here. who is entitled to become a citizen, and where do they stand in line? Because remember, we have millions of people who are standing and waiting in line to become legal citizens of the United States, doing it the proper way, not coming across the border, not enriching cartels. Those people should be the first in line, and we have to have a limit and orderly migration. So I'm afraid deportation is going to be necessary. We're going to have to determine what people What their criminal background is, their ability to actually work, their ability to actually become citizens who agree to our constitutional principles of freedom and individual",
